B. RGDSPSS

All five sequences contain the RGD tripeptide, but in vitro integrin recognition is set by the C-terminal context that allows the fibronectin-type extended β-turn.

The validated high-affinity benchmark is fibronectin type III10 ...GRGDSPASSKP..., minimal active unit RGDSP / GRGDSP. Ser at +1 and Pro at +2/+3 relative to Asp stabilises the extended conformation recognised by α5β1, αvβ3, αvβ5 etc. in solid-phase binding, SPR, cell adhesion and inhibition assays. RGDSP / RGDSPASS and its close variants are the textbook positive controls.

RGDSPSS = RGD-S-P-S-S retains the intact RGDSP core. It is the native RGDSPASS motif with a conservative Ala→Ser change at +4/+5. The critical RGD-Ser-Pro turn, small polar flank, solubility and loop geometry are preserved, so the sequence is functionally equivalent to the documented GRGDSP / RGDSPASS ligand and is highly likely to be a positive binder in vitro.

The alternatives lack the canonical Ser-Pro register:
* A RGDMAA: Met at +1, no Pro in the +2/+3 position;
* C RGDLTTP: Leu at +1 and Pro shifted to +5, steric occlusion and register shift shown to reduce affinity >10-fold;
* D RGDQVSK: Gln-Val at +1/+2, loss of the +1 Ser/+2 Pro turn;
* E RGDARGG: Ala-Arg at +1/+2, disruption of the turn.

Those are the type of low-affinity / negative-control RGD variants used in the literature. Until new binding data emerge for them, RGDSPSS remains the only option that is both mechanistically predicted and experimentally benchmarked as an integrin binder in vitro.

